\pard\b cloudpickle \par
\par
\pard\li720\sl240\slmult1\b0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/cloudpipe/cloudpickle"}}{\fldrslt{\ul\cf1 https://github.com/cloudpipe/cloudpickle}}}\f0\fs20\par
cloudpickle makes it possible to serialize Python constructs not supported by the default pickle module from the Python standard library.\par
cloudpickle is especially useful for cluster computing where Python code is shipped over the network to execute on remote hosts, possibly close to the data.\par
Among other things, cloudpickle supports pickling for lambda functions along with functions and classes defined interactively in the __main__ module (for instance in a script, a shell or a Jupyter notebook).\par
Cloudpickle can only be used to send objects between the exact same version of Python.\par
Using cloudpickle for long-term object storage is not supported and strongly discouraged.\par
Security notice: one should only load pickle data from trusted sources as otherwise pickle.load can lead to arbitrary code execution resulting in a critical security vulnerability.\par
\par
\par
It is similar to pickle serializer, but more capable. Extends python\rquote s \lquote pickle\rquote  module for serializing and de-serializing python objects to the majority of the built-in python types. Has security problems though, just as pickle.\par
\pard\b\par
dill \par
\pard\li720\b0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://dill.readthedocs.io/en/latest/"}}{\fldrslt{\ul\cf1 https://dill.readthedocs.io/en/latest/}}}\f0\fs20\par
\par
dill package documentation\par
dill: serialize all of python\par
About Dill\par
\par
dill extends python\rquote s pickle module for serializing and de-serializing python objects to the majority of the built-in python types. Serialization is the process of converting an object to a byte stream, and the inverse of which is converting a byte stream back to a python object hierarchy.\par
\par
dill provides the user the same interface as the pickle module, and also includes some additional features. In addition to pickling python objects, dill provides the ability to save the state of an interpreter session in a single command. Hence, it would be feasable to save a interpreter session, close the interpreter, ship the pickled file to another computer, open a new interpreter, unpickle the session and thus continue from the \lquote saved\rquote  state of the original interpreter session.\par
\par
dill can be used to store python objects to a file, but the primary usage is to send python objects across the network as a byte stream. dill is quite flexible, and allows arbitrary user defined classes and functions to be serialized. Thus dill is not intended to be secure against erroneously or maliciously constructed data. It is left to the user to decide whether the data they unpickle is from a trustworthy source.\par
\par
dill is part of pathos, a python framework for heterogeneous computing. dill is in active development, so any user feedback, bug reports, comments, or suggestions are highly appreciated. A list of issues is located at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/uqfoundation/dill/issues"}}{\fldrslt{\ul\cf1 https://github.com/uqfoundation/dill/issues}}}\f0\fs20 , with a legacy list maintained at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://uqfoundation.github.io/pathos-issues.html"}}{\fldrslt{\ul\cf1 https://uqfoundation.github.io/pathos-issues.html}}}\f0\fs20 .\par
Major Features\par
\par
dill can pickle the following standard types:\par
\par
        none, type, bool, int, long, float, complex, str, unicode,\par
        tuple, list, dict, file, buffer, builtin,\par
        both old and new style classes,\par
        instances of old and new style classes,\par
        set, frozenset, array, functions, exceptions\par
\par
dill can also pickle more \lquote exotic\rquote  standard types:\par
\par
        functions with yields, nested functions, lambdas,\par
        cell, method, unboundmethod, module, code, methodwrapper,\par
        dictproxy, methoddescriptor, getsetdescriptor, memberdescriptor,\par
        wrapperdescriptor, xrange, slice,\par
        notimplemented, ellipsis, quit\par
\par
dill cannot yet pickle these standard types:\par
\par
        frame, generator, traceback\par
\par
dill also provides the capability to:\par
\par
        save and load python interpreter sessions\par
        save and extract the source code from functions and classes\par
        interactively diagnose pickling errors\par
