\pard\fi-3240\li5040\b\f0 numpy\b0\par
\pard\li2520\tx4320 orjson natively serializes numpy.ndarray instances. Arrays may have a dtype of numpy.bool, numpy.float32, numpy.float64, numpy.int32, numpy.int64, numpy.uint32, numpy.uint64, numpy.uintp, or numpy.intp. orjson is faster than all compared libraries at serializing numpy instances. Serializing numpy data requires specifying option=orjson.OPT_SERIALIZE_NUMPY.\par
>>> import orjson, numpy\par
>>> orjson.dumps(  numpy.array([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]),option=orjson.OPT_SERIALIZE_NUMPY,)\par
b'[[1,2,3],[4,5,6]]\par
The array must be a contiguous C array (C_CONTIGUOUS) and one of the supported datatypes. Individual items (e.g., numpy.float64(1)) are not supported.\par
If an array is not a contiguous C array or contains an supported datatype, orjson falls through to default\par
orjson does not have an installation or compilation dependency on numpy. The implementation is independent, reading numpy.ndarray using PyArrayInterface.\par
\pard\li1800\b\f5\par
API \par
\pard\fi-3240\li5760\cf0 dumps\b0 (  __obj: Any,default: Optional[Callable[[Any], Any]] = ...,option: Optional[int] = ...,) -> bytes: ...\par
\pard\fi-3240\li6480\par
\pard\li3240 It natively serializes str, dict, list, tuple, int, float, bool, dataclasses.dataclass, typing.TypedDict, datetime.datetime, datetime.date, datetime.time, uuid.UUID, numpy.ndarray, and None instances. It supports arbitrary types through default. It does not serialize subclasses of supported types natively, with the exception of dataclasses.dataclass subclasses.\par
The output is a bytes object containing UTF-8.It raises JSONEncodeError \par
\par
\pard\fi-720\li3960 default\tab To serialize a subclass or arbitrary types, specify default as a callable that returns a supported type. default may be a function, lambda, or \ul callable class instance.\ulnone  \par
\tab If a type cannot be handled, defautl MUST raise an exception such as TypeError. Python otherwise implicitly returns None, which appears to the caller like a legitimate value and is serialized:\par
\pard\fi-3240\li6480 option = OPT_XXX | OPT_XXX \par
\pard\fi-3240\li7200 OPT_INDENT_2\tab equivalent to indent=2 in the standard library-\cf3\par
\cf2\f0 OPT_NAIVE_UTC\tab Serialize datetime.datetime objects without a tzinfo as UTC\par
OPT_NON_STR_KEYS\tab Serialize dict keys of type other than str. (str, int, float, bool, None, datetime.datetime, datetime.date, datetime.time, enum.Enum, and uuid.UUID) . Attention c'est un peu plus lent avec l'option\par
OPT_OMIT_MICROSECONDS\tab\tab Do not serialize the microsecond field on datetime.datetime and datetime.time instances.\par
OPT_SERIALIZE_DATACLASS\tab Serialize dataclasses.dataclass instances. For more, see dataclass.\par
OPT_SERIALIZE_NUMPY\tab Serialize numpy.ndarray instances. For more, see numpy.\par
OPT_SERIALIZE_UUID\tab Serialize uuid.UUID instances. For more, see UUID.\par
OPT_SORT_KEYS\tab Serialize dict keys in sorted order. The default is to serialize in an unspecified order. This is equivalent to sort_keys=True in the standard library.\line This can be used to ensure the order is deterministic for hashing or tests. It has a substantial performance penalty and is not recommended in general.\par
OPT_STRICT_INTEGER\tab Enforce 53-bit limit on integers. The limit is otherwise 64 bits, the same as the Python standard library. For more, see int.\par
OPT_UTC_Z\tab Serialize a UTC timezone on datetime.datetime instances as Z instead of +00:00.\par
\pard\fi-3240\li6480\par
\pard\li2520\b loads\b0 (__obj: Union[bytes, bytearray, str]) -> Any: ...\par
\pard\li3240 loads() deserializes JSON to Python objects. It deserializes to dict, list, int, float, str, bool, and None objects.\par
bytes, bytearray, and str input are accepted. If the input exists as bytes (was read directly from a source), it is recommended to pass bytes. This has lower memory usage and lower latency.\par
The input must be valid UTF-8.\b\par
